Distinct components: product card (logo, name, one-liner, star rating, review count, price-was/now, urgency badge), hero email-capture form, trust-icon strip (3-icon row), horizontal "View all" link pattern, mega-nav dropdown (New arrivals / Ending soon / Radar), cart icon with counter, footer mega-menu (5 sections: Account, AppSumo, Sell, Learn, Social), "Part of the Sumo family" cross-sell module (TidyCal, BreezeDoc, FormRobin, SendFox), and a "Login" utility nav item.

### 1.8 Design Strengths & Weaknesses
| Strengths | Weaknesses |
|---|---|
| Strong, ownable mascot/brand personality distinct from generic marketplace look | Homepage still fairly text/price-dense once past the hero — could better spotlight "why this deal" storytelling |
| Consistent, scannable card component across every grid | Countdown urgency ("Ends in an hour") is text-only on homepage — a live timer would likely lift urgency-driven conversion further |
| Clear trust bar immediately below hero (guarantee, customer count, curation claim) | Limited visual differentiation between "Top 9" and "Just launched" sections beyond the header label |
| No disabled pinch-zoom — better mobile accessibility than category peers | Star-rating/review-count pairing repeated twice in the HTML per card (minor redundancy, possibly an SSR/hydration artifact) |

**Actionable Takeaways:**
- The trust-bar-directly-under-hero pattern (curation + guarantee + social proof, in three icons) is a highly transferable, low-effort trust-building pattern for any marketplace.
- A custom mascot is a strong differentiator in a category (SaaS deals) that otherwise looks generic — worth the illustration investment for brand recall.
- Converting text-only urgency badges to live countdown timers is a low-cost, high-leverage CRO test.

